The Symbol: "The Throne"
The visual symbol of Post-Perception is **"The Throne"** – a solid oak chair designed with precise geometric symmetry so that it remains fully functional even when turned upside down. It asks: *"Is what we see enough to know what a thing is?"*

Key Work: "Isolation" (عزل)
Selected for the Egypt Pavilion at the **Malta Biennale 2026**.
**Description:** An installation reconstructing a hospital isolation room. Walls and floor are covered with pharmaceutical leaflets. A large circle made of 550 empty medicine boxes dominates the wall. A narrow bed holds a decaying sculpted head, from which 920 pills flow like blood through fragile arteries. A faint heartbeat fills the room. The smell of surgical disinfectant lingers.

**Concept:** The work does not dramatize suffering. It slows perception to a point where the body is experienced as architecture – fragile, repetitive, finite. It asks: *What remains of a human being when time, relationships, and movement are taken away?*

**Materials:** Discarded medicine boxes, pharmaceutical leaflets, sculpted head, bed, sound system, scent diffuser.

**Significance:** The work was born from Shalabi's personal experience of medical isolation during the COVID-19 pandemic. It marks the transition from his earlier figurative work to the conceptual framework of Post-Perception.

Philosophical Roots
Post-Perception builds on both Western and Arab-Islamic philosophy:
| Tradition | Key Figures | Contribution |
|-----------|-------------|--------------|
| **Western** | Plato, Aristotle, Descartes, Kant, Husserl, Merleau-Ponty, Foucault, Baudrillard, Deleuze | Critique of perception, phenomenology, simulation theory |
| **Arab-Islamic** | Al-Ghazali (critique of the senses), Ibn Sina (hierarchy of perception), Ibn al-Haytham (scientific analysis of vision), Ibn Arabi (mystical transcendence) | Early skepticism toward the senses, embodied perception, spiritual dimensions |
This synthesis gives the movement both universal relevance and cultural specificity.

